  "narration": {
    "title": "The Arms Race Runs Through Milwaukee",
    "intro": "Today the column belongs to the pitchers. Milwaukee has the best rotation in the game and a bullpen in the top five, which is a big reason it keeps hanging with the Dodgers atop the standings. The Yankees, meanwhile, are quietly building the best relief corps in baseball.",
    "staff_lead": "The Brewers own the lowest rotation ERA at 3.40, a hair ahead of the Dodgers at 3.41 and Tampa Bay at 3.44. Milwaukee also carries the cleanest walk-and-hit rate of the group at a 1.09 WHIP, the mark of a staff that rarely gives free bases.",
    "bullpen_lead": "The Yankees lead all bullpens at a 3.04 ERA, with the Braves a step behind at 3.06 and Boston third at 3.21. A relief group that reliable is why New York looks like a real threat, not just a lineup that mashes.",
    "arms_lead": "Jacob Misiorowski of the Brewers still sits at the top with a 1.62 ERA and a 0.76 WHIP, form barely off its ceiling at 99. Cam Schlittler gives the Yankees another elite arm at a 2.05 ERA, while Chris Sale keeps humming along at 2.20 for Atlanta.",
    "risers_lead": "Joe Ryan and Jesus Luzardo each gained five points of form this week, Ryan up to 75 and Luzardo to 60. Modest bumps, but both are trending the right way while a few bigger names cooled."
  }
}